docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:1468:| G10 / manifest = authority tuple | How do authority values authenticate `alg`, `issuer` and `ver`, absent from the TXT/blob? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:359-362`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:362-369`, `:569-576`; `formal/p8/PROOF.md:79-84` | **Depends on OPEN.** Receipt/manifest equality is explicit, but the M5 channel-authentication condition is not. | Implement the R:83 equality, stop at authority composition. Same keys/window with a different well-formed algorithm or issuer tuple could satisfy fingerprint-only channel checks and fail a stricter authority-tuple rule. This does not license arbitrary unsupported algorithms; their independent check still applies. | **yes** |
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:1470:| G12 / algorithm policy | What governing specification hash, frozen signer branch and full allowed/prohibited identifier policy is installed? | `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:146-148`, `:268-278`, `:386-391`, `:523`; `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:83-85`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:438-442`, `:480-489` | **Depends on OPEN / silent.** Classical id is concrete; branch/id/supplement and original prohibited-id cases are not furnished. | Parse classical O and unknown opaque slots only; no PQ registration assumed. A classical receipt can pass a fallback profile and fail a hybrid-required profile; an eventual pair id cannot be guessed from ML-DSA-65's name. Governing policy must be installed independently, not supplied by receipt content. | **yes** |
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:1474:| G16 / DNSSEC algorithm and validation profile | Which exact DNSSEC algorithms/editions, canonical signed-data rules, delegation handling and unsupported-algorithm outcomes govern? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:290-295`, `:398-407`, `:436`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:141`, `:158`, `:307-323` | **Silent here / external dependency.** Root existential rule and selected witness timing are concrete, but the full DNSSEC verifier profile and normative dependency closure are absent. | Stop; no network/RFC lookup or library used to fill policy. An algorithm in the historical chain supported by one validator and unsupported by another changes performability; an extra invalid RRSIG must not defeat a valid witness (P:403-406 resolves that part). | **yes** |
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:1488:| G25 / git parser and signature profile | How are duplicate headers, folded gpgsig, multiple signatures, armored payloads, duplicate tree entries and noncanonical tree objects handled? Which repository signing algorithms are supported? | `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:692-711`; `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:314-341`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:295-305`, `:326-328` | **Silent here / external dependency.** The raw hash formula, path/modes and signature-removal purpose are supplied; complete git object/signature grammar and repo key scheme are not. | Stop; no subprocess git/gpg or existing implementation consulted. First-versus-last duplicate path/header selection can change authenticated blob interpretation. A robust implementation needs a declared grammar/duplicate policy. A9 settles sha1-only descriptor and exact stamped hex+LF; those are not open gaps. | **yes** |
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:1489:| G26 / all-failure aggregation | How are dependent failed versus unperformable checks collected without order affecting verdict? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:140-161`, `:168-174`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:508-511`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:179-180`, `:285` | **Silent execution contract.** Final precedence is clear, but some codec decodes stop at an unknown algorithm or absent member before other independent failures can be found. | Probe deliberately reports a local stop/rejection, not V1 aggregation. A short-circuit unknown-algorithm implementation can return UNVERIFIABLE where another independently finds a bad slot/schema and returns INVALID. The correct final precedence is specified; implementing it needs dependency-aware checks. | **yes** |
