docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:1955:**Location:** draft §D/G02, lines 438–458; §B/G03; E §7.1; A9 C4/C5 and A9.6 chain-time residual.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:1993:This is not a demonstrated unsafe accept under A9: A9's undefined “earliest” is the issue. Pruning reproduces either selected attestation but does not make two different algorithms identical on the **same declared input**. “Smallest nTime mixes blocks” is not a valid objection: that implementation can take both T and confirmed_at from the attestation selected by smallest nTime.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:2023:The algorithm allowlist is a legitimate visible NARROW-CLAIM, not a demonstrated NARROW-MODEL. Unsupported algorithms must never become an insecure-but-accepted chain. However, the table “per required RRset” says a supported signature validating at T_snap passes and does not include KSK reliance eligibility. G22 supplies byte equality, not the missing period check. Another implementer will import A9's period rule; a literal implementation of the generalized table can pass a root signed by a trusted retired KSK outside reliance. A captured retired KSK can produce such a signature without breaking its algorithm. A9 governs and forbids this acceptance, so the final text must expressly preserve it.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:2027:The observed root/com algorithm 8 and wamason.com algorithm 13 support the bounded current-chain choice. Dropping instrument support for DS type 4 has no demonstrated security benefit under A9's cryptographic assumptions and can reject honest future chain configurations. Algorithm 13 is already allowed, so a root move to ECDSA P-256/SHA-256 **alone** does not cause the example's unsupported outcome. A new unlisted KSK is a separate trust-coverage problem.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:2033:Define DS outcomes explicitly: a supported matching authenticated DS/DNSKEY path is eligible; if no such path exists and an unsupported algorithm/digest could supply the required path, report UNVERIFIABLE/DNSSEC_ALGORITHM_UNSUPPORTED; if all relevant possibilities are supported and checked and none matches, report INVALID/DNSKEY_DS_MISMATCH. Missing necessary RRsets/signatures are unperformable. Preserve P's root reliance/window/crypto reason precedence. Add mixed type-2/type-4, supported+unsupported RRSIG, missing witness, and outside-reliance controls. Replace the rollover example with an actually unsupported algorithm. Record the allowlist as an owner scope choice, with issue-time fail-before-ship handling. No P8 change is required; an amendment is required only if this overrides A9's root rule or registered honest-case scope rather than selecting a supported issuance path.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:2085:**Location:** draft G21 lines 167–178; A9.6; E §5.2.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:2087:After allowing an ML-DSA-65 SPKI for kms, G21 says “Each must parse as P-256: algorithm 19 …”. A literal implementer rejects every hybrid kms key. Another applies the implied exception. A9 authorizes the hybrid; both readings cannot govern.
