docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:5685:+**Answer: not yet.** A9 + the 1045-line V1-PROFILE + ENCODING-V1 now specify substantially more of a verifier, including concrete container, snapshot, git and OTS formats. They still do not determine a unique verdict and six-field report on every input. There are acknowledged freeze inputs still to supply, and additional forks in algorithm routing, nested absence, DNSSEC candidate classification, proof checks and reason ordering. Section 14.2 is not complete.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:5704:+### F01 — High: algorithm policy, codec routing and M5 do not compose
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:5708:+Three rules disagree at their join. E chooses the signature codec from each frame's own `common.alg`, and recognizes the classical id as rule O. P says an id other than the frozen catalog id, including the unchosen branch, is `UNVERIFIABLE / ALG_UNKNOWN`; P's aggregation paragraph says an unknown alg routes the slot as opaque. M5 separately says manifest `alg` equals the catalog's single id and mismatches are INVALID. Literal M5 enforcement can therefore turn the intended unknown-algorithm canary into INVALID even when both frames and manifest agree on the same well-formed unknown id. On a hybrid installation, a classical id can select rule O under E or opaque handling under P; non-rule-O signature bytes distinguish those codecs. Other independent failures may still make either complete bundle INVALID; the routing fork exists before them.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:5712:+> Each frame is decoded with E §5.2's codec registry, including any authorized supplement, using its own alg and slot. A codec-known but unselected branch still uses its specified codec; an id absent from that registry uses the opaque codec. Signature/possession verification semantics are performed only for the catalog-supported id. Receipt/manifest alg equality is checked independently for every syntactically valid id. M5's catalog-alg condition is performable only for a policy-supported id; unsupported ids contribute ALG_UNKNOWN, not an additional SCHEMA failure.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:5756:+RFC 4035 §5.3.1 requires matching signer, algorithm and key tag, a usable Zone Key, and Labels constraints, among other checks. The profile says to perform that validation, but gives result classes only for missing evidence, potentially supported paths, time failure, crypto failure and DS mismatch. An intact TXT RRset with a supported RRSIG whose tag matches **no** DNSKEY is not a missing RRSIG, not a checked crypto failure, and not a child-DNSKEY/DS mismatch. Labels greater than the owner count, protocol/flag defects, and supported malformed key/signature encodings have similarly missing outcomes. “Unsupported ... could supply the path” does not define which mismatching/unauthenticated candidates count as potentially usable. One implementation can call an unmatched selector missing evidence, another INVALID, another mislabel it DNSKEY_DS_MISMATCH. [RFC 4035 §5.3.1](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4035.html#section-5.3.1).
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:5758:+**Exact fix:** supply a table mapping every supported validation precondition failure to a code/class, and separate DS→DNSKEY matching from RRset→RRSIG matching. Proposed entries: malformed supported key/RRSIG RDATA = INVALID/DNSSEC_FORMAT; present supported witness with no matching eligible DNSKEY = INVALID/DNSSEC_KEY_MISMATCH; Labels too large or other non-temporal metadata mismatch = INVALID/DNSSEC_RRSIG_METADATA; absent required RRset/RRSIG = UNVERIFIABLE/DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING. Define “potentially unsupported path” by authenticated DS metadata and matching algorithm/tag/name selectors, not any unrelated unsupported RR. If insufficient evidence is intended to cover an unmatched key instead, select that classification expressly and test it.
docs/reviews/v1-profile-r2/codex.log:5880:+**Locations:** P:29, 47–49, 658–660, 825–872. A9.6 slot-2 deadline; RB §6.
